I often refer to programs as "my" program, but (usually) not in the way that the article warns about, with my ego being tied to it; rather, as I wrote
the program, it's simply my program as a fact. 

The experiment the author brought up is pretty interesting, though it probably wouldn't work on me for the reasons said above.
But who knows? Maybe there's some remnant of possessive ego lodged deep inside me that I have no idea about till I poke at it. 

MUSING4
Not exactly. If I got to the point of calling it "my" program, that would imply that I put in pretty much all of the work to bring said code to fruition,
and would imply that others haven't really touched it all that much. On the other hand, if others *did* collaborate, that wouldn't be "my" program, would it?

If I make a typo or any stupid mistake like that, that's on me. Unless the computer's keys are wired differently, but I haven't come across one of those yet.

MUSING5
Coding has nothing to do with luck; every decision is deliberate. I don't blame bad luck for any of my own mistakes.
I believe I could answer this by mentioning the many times where a bug popped up in my code close to a submission deadline, but the bad luck would pertain
to the timing of the revelation, rather than the mistake itself.

Notes:
- Article has an undertone of frustration and anger against those who exhibit "aggressive" personality traits. Author fails to consider that many who desire
status and wealth do not desire these qualities as an end but rather a means to a greater end (true fulfillment). 
The manager's job is inherently less fulfilling (for most people) than the programmer's --> managers internalize philosophy of work as a means rather than an end -->
applies said philosophy to others --> BAD THINGS HAPPEN.
This discussion revolves around two worldviews (work as the means to fulfillment and work as fulfillment itself) clashing; author himself commits the same mistake
of being too universalistic in his Theory of Life by presuming that an orientation towards money / prestige reflects lower cooperativeness